Title: Koka
[INCLUDE=../sidebar-sub.mdk]






# Minimal but General { .heading-no-number }

The name Koka (効果) comes from the Japanese word for _effect_ -- a fitting name
for a language whose central idea is tracking and handling effects.

Koka has a small core of orthogonal, well-studied language features — first-class
functions, a polymorphic type- and effect system, algebraic data types, and effect
handlers. Each of these is as general and _composable_ as possible, so the language
avoids needing "special" extensions for each new use case.

Where Haskell distinguishes itself through pure-vs-monadic programming and Rust
through borrow checking, Koka distinguishes itself through _effect typing_,
_effect handlers_, and _Perceus_ memory management. These three features work
together: effect types make programs easier to reason about, handlers make
them composable, and Perceus makes them fast.

## Control Flow as Function Calls

```koka {.aside}
fun hello-ten()
  var i := 0
  while { i < 10 }
    println("hello")
    i := i + 1
```

As an example of this _min-gen_ design principle, Koka implements most
control-flow primitives as regular function calls. An anonymous function
without arguments can be written using just braces, as `{ <body> }`.
Moreover, using brace elision, any indented block automatically gets curly braces.

We can write a `while` loop using regular function calls as shown in the
example, where the call to `while` is desugared to
`while( fn(){ i < 10 }, fn(){ ... } )`.

This naturally leads to _consistency_: an expression between _parentheses_
is always evaluated before a function call, whereas an expression between
_braces_ (ah, _suspenders_!) is suspended and may never be evaluated or
evaluated more than once (as in our `while` predicate). This is
inconsistent in most other languages where the predicate of a `while` loop
is written in parentheses but may be evaluated many times.

## Dot Selection

In Koka, `x.f(y)` is exactly the same as `f(x, y)`. This makes it easy to
extend any type with new "methods" — just write a new function whose first
parameter has that type. In most object-oriented languages you would need to
modify the class definition itself.

```koka
fun main()
  "hello world".to-upper.println
  // Equivalent to: println(to-upper("hello world"))
```

## The `with` Statement

The `with` statement transforms nested callback-style code into a linear
sequence of statements, making effect handlers and resource management read
naturally:

```koka
effect hello-effect
  fun hello() : ()
fun hello-with()
  with handler
    fun hello() println("hello from handler!")
  hello()
```
